ays":"R1/09 and Recommendation no. 2/2010 are both non-binding guidelines, but the social-tariff scenarios studied are based specifically on R1/09. The paper says R1/09 is “simply a set of guidelines,” while the conclusion identifies evaluating the combined effect of R1/09 and Recommendation no. 2/2010 as future work; R2/10 concerns tariff-calculation criteria rather than the analyzed social-tariff design.","where":"pp. 2–3, Introduction; p. 8, ERSAR; p. 23, Conclusion"},"effect_measure":{"agrees":"Y","paper_says":"The methodology compares S0 (no social tariffs), S1 (actual social tariffs), and S2 (R1/09 social tariffs), reporting measures including “Total subsidy volume,” “Subsidy per beneficiary,” and bill accrual for non-beneficiaries.","where":"pp. 17–18, Methodology, Tables 4–5; p. 19, Table 6"},"effect_estimate":{"agrees":"N","paper_says":"The broad conclusions and EUR 19.11 average, EUR 2.53–40.52 range, and EUR 37.99 spread are supported. However, existing social tariffs were not quantified across all 278 municipalities: actual-ST samples were 101 municipalities for WP, 77 for WT, and 68 for UWM. For all 278 municipalities, the analysis compares the no-ST baseline with synthetic R1/09 tariffs using potential users. The study also assumes the upper-bound threshold of EUR 13,580 rather than treating it merely as a cap.","where":"p. 5, Table 2; pp. 8–9, Concept of Social Tariffs; p. 17, Table 3; pp. 17–19, Methodology and Table 6"},"extraction_note":{"agrees":"cannot_tell","paper_says":"The paper itself does not contain the review-system identifier “RA7E07382F0E4.”","where":"Not reported in the paper"}},"overall_comment":"Most high-level descriptors are supported, but the service-provider and effect-estimate entries conflate municipalities with providers and overstate the coverage of the actual-social-tariff analysis. R2/10 was contextual and proposed for future combined analysis, whereas the modeled social-tariff recommendation was R1/09."}
